body:
Confirmed on 72fcb93ef3ae99a5f9fe769a51ea0d50ef702b97. 

Nodes n1 and n2 can communicate with each other while connections to n3 are blocked with this policy:

```json
{
  "groups": {
    "group:admin": ["alice@"]
  },
  "tagOwners": {
    "tag:foo": [
      "group:admin"
    ]
  },
  "acls": [
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["group:admin"],
      "dst": ["*:*"]
    },
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["autogroup:self:*"]
    }
  ]
}
```

```
$ headscale nodes list -t
ID | Hostname | Name | MachineKey | NodeKey | User           | IP addresses                  | Ephemeral | Last seen           | Expiration | Connected | Expired | ForcedTags | InvalidTags | ValidTags
1  | n1       | n1   | [utXwe]    | [3cter] | alice          | 100.64.0.1, fd7a:115c:a1e0::1 | false     | 2026-01-11 09:43:09 | N/A        | online    | no      |            |             |          
2  | n2       | n2   | [2zuIW]    | [ujjXu] | alice          | 100.64.0.2, fd7a:115c:a1e0::2 | false     | 2026-01-11 09:43:08 | N/A        | online    | no      |            |             |          
3  | n3       | n3   | [1eWoG]    | [pk8CP] | tagged-devices | 100.64.0.3, fd7a:115c:a1e0::3 | false     | 2026-01-11 09:43:07 | N/A        | online    | no      | tag:foo    |             |  
```

Connections to n3 start to work once the `autogroup:self` stanza is removed.

Yup, specifically
```json
{
  "groups": {
    "group:admin": ["user"]
  },
  "tagOwners": {
    "tag:admin": ["group:admin"]
  },
  "acls": [
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["group:admin"],
      "dst": ["*:*"]
    },
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["autogroup:self:*"]
    }
  ],
}
```

Okay, apologies for the delayed reply. I was able to reproduce it with a minimal example on #3044. I got
- one user `user1`
- two nodes
	- `node1`: `100.64.0.1`, user device, MacOS
	- `node2`: `100.64.0.2`, tagged with `tag:admin`, iPadOS

with the following ACL:
```json
{
  "groups": {
    "group:admin": ["user1@"]
  },
  "tagOwners": {
    "tag:admin": ["group:admin"]
  },
  "acls": [
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["group:admin"],
      "dst": ["*:*"]
    },
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["autogroup:self:*"]
    }
  ]
}
```

`node1` cannot reach `node2` at all, with this netmap output: [node1.json](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/24866554/node1.json).
*However*, interestingly `node2` can reach `node1`, which seems unexpected since, afaik, this policy shouldn't allow that. That has me thinking that perhaps some rule is applied/evaluated the wrong way around here. Unfortunately, since it's on an iPad, I don't have a shell to grab that debug output.


Edit: looking back at the test in #3026, the only difference I can see is that in my case both devices belong to the same user. In the test they belong to different users. I thought with tagged-devices that would become meaningless anyways, but maybe not?
